--- Part E (item 3): explicit double-index Watson's-lemma re-derivation ---

Standard Watson's lemma: for g(v) smooth at v=0,
   int_0^inf e^{-v/eps} g(v) dv  ~  sum_{j>=0} g^{(j)}(0) * eps^{j+1}
(since int_0^inf e^{-v/eps} v^j dv = eps^{j+1} * j!, and g's j-th Taylor
coefficient is g^{(j)}(0)/j!, the j! cancels).

Applying this to g(v) = chi_n(v) = (gamma_n-gamma_{n-1})*R^{(n-1)}(v) gives
   int_0^inf e^{-v/eps} chi_n(v) dv ~ sum_{j>=0} (gamma_n-gamma_{n-1}) *
                                       R^{(n-1+j)}(0) * eps^{j+1}

Pi(c) = (1/eps) * int_0^inf e^{-v/eps} * sum_n eps^n chi_n(v) dv
      = sum_n eps^{n-1} * sum_j (gamma_n-gamma_{n-1}) R^{(n-1+j)}(0) eps^{j+1}
      = sum_n sum_j eps^{n+j} (gamma_n-gamma_{n-1}) R^{(n-1+j)}(0)

Collect coefficient of eps^N (N = n+j, j = N-n >= 0, so n=1..N):
   coeff(eps^N) = sum_{n=1}^N (gamma_n-gamma_{n-1}) * R^{(n-1+(N-n))}(0)
                = sum_{n=1}^N (gamma_n-gamma_{n-1}) * R^{(N-1)}(0)
   [the exponent n-1+(N-n) = N-1 is INDEPENDENT OF n -- this is exactly
    why R^{(N-1)}(0) factors out of the n-sum in the target's Sec 2.3]
                = R^{(N-1)}(0) * sum_{n=1}^N (gamma_n-gamma_{n-1})
                = R^{(N-1)}(0) * (gamma_N - gamma_0) = gamma_N * R^{(N-1)}(0)
   [telescoping sum, gamma_0:=0 -- confirmed trivial but exact]

Mechanical check, N=1..4 (published Pi(c) 4-term-law coefficients, i.e.
Pi(c) = sum_N eps^N * [this coefficient]):

